# status section
d.rep(r"""Take as selector the map that assigns to each agent the source of smallest reciprocal row cost \cref{erase:peer-cost} at the current state, and as handoff the closure time $\tau$. The admitted class is open in the invariant family \cref{erase:family}, so (F1) holds in its invariant-family form. Selection with a persistence criterion, (F2), holds: the box pairs are an invariant block relation after $\tau$, and $\tau$ is a function of the state. Inheritance, (F3), holds for the declared public response class through \cref{erase:parent-flow}, with the qualification that the parent state is an empirical measure rather than a law pair. Compression, (F4), fails: the retention is non-injective only on private and gate coordinates that no admitted probe reads, and it keeps every public child state. Recursion, (F6), fails, since there is a single stage and the two final boxes stay separated.""",
      r"""We report the construction by the scheme following \cref{inh:recursive-formation}. The objective is the exact Gibbs-reduced reciprocal potential \cref{eq:dyn-reduced}, and the dynamics is its Fisher flow on finite positive tables, restricted to the invariant product family \cref{erase:family}. (F1) holds in the family grade: the admitted class of \cref{erase:gate-entry} and \cref{erase:pairs} is relatively open in that family, which has twenty-four means against fifty-six table coordinates, so its codimension is thirty-two; openness in the full table chart is not shown. The selector is declared as follows. At each time each agent's preferred source in each sector is the source of smallest reciprocal row cost \cref{erase:peer-cost}, computed from the original formulas at the current state, all minimizers being kept at a tie; the selected partition consists of the connected components of the graph that joins every agent to its preferred sources in either sector. The family has no real constant. The persistence criterion is the invariant block relation of the box pairs after $\tau$, and the handoff is the closure time $\tau$, a function of the state; (F2) holds. The parent protocol rule $A_I$ consists of the fresh public responses whose source selection depends only on public states, including joint responses with a shared sampled child; the child-level probe rule $B_I$ consists of the peer channels of \cref{erase:compared-law} applied to each member, whose public output reads the member's public mean and whose private output reads its private mean through the gate, together with the protocols of $A_J$ for the merged singletons. Inheritance, (F3), holds for $A_I$ through \cref{erase:parent-flow}, with the retention of each pair taken to be the pair of retained measures and with the qualification that the parent state is an empirical measure rather than a law pair.

Compression, (F4), holds in the letter of the clause through the label quotient only. The private and gate coordinates that the retention discards provide no witness: on the reachable post-handoff set the gates are closed, every gated private output has law $b_0$, and no probe of $B_I$ reads those coordinates. Within a pair, however, the empirical measure forgets which member carries which public state, and the reachable set contains, with a configuration whose box-mates are distinct, the configuration in which their public states are exchanged; the peer channel applied to one of them separates the two. The witness is transient, since box-mates converge by \cref{erase:pair-convergence}, and it is of symmetry type, since the fibers are orbits of within-pair permutations, which are symmetries of the fine field and of the protocols of $A_I$. Recursion, (F6), fails, since there is a single stage and the two final boxes stay separated.""")
d.rep(r"""For the null-flow control of \cref{inh:null-flow}, delete every peer cost. The public coordinates then follow the single-agent flows, which keep the two sign boxes invariant by \cref{erase:well-margins}; the private means relax monotonically toward zero without changing sign; and the gate means follow \cref{erase:closed-clock} with the factor two replaced by one, so the gates still close. The same selector, evaluated along this flow, therefore reads the crossed pairs while the private advantage dominates and the box pairs after it is erased. The sequence of selected pairings coincides with that of the reciprocal flow, and only the switching time changes; the null gates relax at about half the speed. Clause (F5) is therefore met only through a shift of an event time, and its strong form fails: the final pairing is the sign pattern of the initial public states and the initial pairing is the sign pattern of the initial private states, and the null flow preserves both. The construction is a single-stage selection with exact inheritance by a decoupled-factor projection, not a two-stage selection and not a recursive formation.""",
      r"""For the controls of \cref{inh:null-flow}, every cost \cref{erase:peer-cost} is a peer cost, because its compared law reads the source's public and private means, and the source sets exclude the receiver, so the label-preserving and renormalized nulls coincide. Deleting the peer costs leaves the local terms. The public coordinates then follow the single-agent flows, which keep the two sign boxes invariant by \cref{erase:well-margins}; the private means relax monotonically toward zero without changing sign; and the gate means follow \cref{erase:closed-clock} with the factor two replaced by one, so the gates still close, at about half the speed. The selector reads the original costs at the null state. Both flows start from the same state, so their first selected partitions agree; on the explicit crossed preparation it is the partition into crossed pairs. After closure along each flow, the private costs of a row do not depend on the source and the public states lie in the boxes, so by \cref{erase:cost-comparison} both flows end with the partition into box pairs. Between these, the four agents need not change their preferred sources at the same instant, and the intermediate partitions, which depend on the order of those changes, are not determined by the results proved here. Clause (F5) is therefore neither established nor shown to fail: $P$ and $P^0$ have the same first and last partitions, and their equality is not proved. Its strong grade is not met on the explicit preparation, where the first and last partitions are fixed by the initial private and public sign patterns, which both flows preserve. No selector-free discrepancy beyond the closure times is established. The frozen-environment control holds the other agents' laws, and with them the gate statistic of each agent's own force, at their initial values; its partition sequence is not determined here. The construction is a single-stage selection with exact inheritance by a decoupled-factor projection, with (F5) undetermined; it is not a two-stage selection and not a recursive formation.""")
